                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "238844197",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198353864392",
                            "num_reviews": 12,
                            "playtime_forever": 1457,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 1457,
                            "last_played": 1789945022
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1] Psychonauts 2 [/h1]\r\n[h2] A Beautiful glimpse into the Human Mind and Passion [/h2]\r\n\r\n[h3] Initial Thoughts [/h3]\r\nBefore I begin I would like to say that I have played Psychonauts 2 before when it first came out on the Xbox, having heard and seen playthroughs on the first game but never had personality had the experience of playing it. Then when Psychonauts 2 was announced and released for Console something continuned drawing me back to the game.\r\n\r\n[h3] Art Style [/h3]\r\nThe first thing I would like to talk about is the exotic and eye capturing design of Psychonauts 2 art style, the game possessing a design that blends whimsical cartoon proportions with an almost claymation-inspired art design. Not to mention the gorgeously crafted psychological dreamscapes of each level, using great imagery and symbolism to ensure no level feels the same.\r\n\r\n[h3] Game Play [/h3]\r\nWhat I love about Psychonauts 2 is that they don't waste time in giving you [u] majority [/u] of your previously learned abilities from the first game, jumping straight into giving you new and excited psychic abilities that further how you play and explore the psychic mindscape and the open world environment. [b] However [/b] I will note that giving you majority of your previous psychic abilities gives a con of Psychonauts 2 lacking in the new abilities that are unlocked. [spoiler] Only unlocking a total of 3 new psychic abilities compared to the first game's 8. Abet this is understandable as in the first Psychonauts you unlock basic gameplay abilities that would be tedious to go through again in the second game. [/spoiler]\r\n\r\nBesides this, the platforming, character movement, and camera of Psychonauts 2 feels leagues better than the original. A not surprising revelation given the 16+ year difference between the two games, but a nice improvement nonetheless given how some modern games fail to capture such fluid movement and controls.\r\n\r\n[h3] Story [/h3]\r\nI think the story and plot of Psychonauts 2 serves as one of the game's strongest focal points and appeals. Further expanding on the Psychonaut world and giving player's more insight to questions we still had from the first game\r\n\r\n[b] Warning! I will be discussing Game Spoilers from here! [/b]\r\n[spoiler] First is how the game handles interactions with bodies of water, the Aquato curse of the Hand of Galochio acting as a clever way for Raz to avoid any large pools of water and out of bounds areas. Only later to discover that the curse was never real and instead a implanted manifestation created by Ford Cruller. Thus after completing the Main story of Psychonauts 2, whenever you fall into bodies of water, instead of grabbing Raz and pulling him under, the Hand of Galochio instead pulls Raz out of the water and no longer causes any Damage to him. [/spoiler]\r\n\r\n[h3] Story/Level Design/Trauma [/h3]\r\n[spoiler] Next is how the game handles different types of traumas and coping mechanisms through there use of level designs. One of my favourite levels of this being Bob's Bottles where we see Bob Zanotto deals with the aftermath of the fight with Maligula but also with losing Helmut through drinking. His level covered in themes of both plant life and alcohol glasses/bottles. His internal character stuck on a literal island surrounded by water showing Bob's own self isolation as he refuses to plant and garden anymore. This also revealing Bob's refusal to grow human connections anymore after he lost Helmut. [/spoiler]\r\n\r\n[h3] Story/Level Design/Trauma Pt2 [/h3]\r\n[spoiler] The same can be shown through another level of the Psychic Seven, Cassie O'Pia. The famous Psychonaut author developing a strong defensive split-personality/compartmentalization in her psychic mind which is shown through it being a Library filled with multiple paper Cassie's each claiming to be the \"Real Cassie\". A unique play to show how Cassie uses writer personas to shield herself from past rejection and overwhelming expectations. Psychonauts 2 being filled with much more of these clever types of representation such as how Maligula was developed through a form of Fight or Flight response, or Ford's own Guilt and dissociation, leading to his fragmented identity [/spoiler]\r\n\r\n[h3] Closing Thoughts [/h3]\r\nIf you were a fan of the original Psychonauts, or enjoy unique and solid platforming games with a good story. Then I highly suggest you pick up Psychonauts 2 and give it a shot. Strongly believing that there is something for everyone in this game, ultimately seeing Psychonauts 2 as not only just a fancy puzzle platformer, but a game about representing trauma, healing, and of course, dentistry.",
